
{
 "cells": [
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# LeetCode 22: Generate Parentheses\n",
    "\n",
    "**Difficulty:** Medium  \n",
    "**Topics:** Backtracking, Dynamic Programming, Recursion  \n",
    "**Language:** Python 3\n",
    "\n",
    "## Problem Statement\n",
    "\n",
    "Given n pairs of parentheses, write a function to generate all combinations of well-formed parentheses.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Example 1\n",
    "Input: n = 3  \n",
    "Output: [\"((()))\", \"(()())\", \"(())()\", \"()(())\", \"()()()\"]\n",
    "\n",
    "### Example 2\n",
    "Input: n = 1  \n",
    "Output: [\"()\"]\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Approach 1: Brute Force + Validation\n",
    "\n",
    "### Intuition\n",
    "\n",
    "Generate all possible strings of length 2*n using opening and closing parentheses. Then check which strings are valid.\n",
    "\n",
    "A string is valid if:\n",
    "- The balance never becomes negative.\n",
    "- The final balance is zero.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Algorithm\n",
    "1. Generate all possible strings of length 2*n.\n",
    "2. Validate each generated string.\n",
    "3. If the string is valid, append it to the result list.\n",
    "4. Return the result list."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "class Solution:\n",
    "    def generateParenthesis(self, n: int) -> list[str]:\n",
    "        result = []\n",
    "\n",
    "        def isValid(s):\n",
    "            balance = 0\n",
    "\n",
    "            for char in s:\n",
    "                if char == '(':\n",
    "                    balance += 1\n",
    "                else:\n",
    "                    balance -= 1\n",
    "\n",
    "                if balance < 0:\n",
    "                    return False\n",
    "\n",
    "            return balance == 0\n",
    "\n",
    "        def generate(s):\n",
    "            if len(s) == 2 * n:\n",
    "                if isValid(s):\n",
    "                    result.append(s)\n",
    "                return\n",
    "\n",
    "            generate(s + '(')\n",
    "            generate(s + ')')\n",
    "\n",
    "        generate('')\n",
    "        return result\n",
    "\n",
    "# Test\n",
    "sol = Solution()\n",
    "print(sol.generateParenthesis(3))"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Complexity Analysis\n",
    "\n",
    "- Time Complexity: O(n * 4^n), because there are 2^(2n) possible strings and each validation takes O(n).\n",
    "- Auxiliary Space Complexity: O(n) for recursion depth, excluding the output.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Limitation\n",
    "\n",
    "This approach generates many invalid strings and checks them afterward. It wastes time exploring combinations that could have been rejected earlier."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Approach 2: Backtracking (Optimal)\n",
    "\n",
    "### Intuition\n",
    "\n",
    "Instead of generating all possible strings, generate only valid combinations.\n",
    "\n",
    "We maintain two counters:\n",
    "- open_count: Number of opening brackets used.\n",
    "- close_count: Number of closing brackets used.\n",
    "\n",
    "Rules:\n",
    "1. Add '(' if open_count < n.\n",
    "2. Add ')' if close_count < open_count.\n",
    "3. When the string length becomes 2*n, add it to the result.\n",
    "\n",
    "The condition close_count < open_count ensures that a closing bracket never appears before its matching opening bracket.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Algorithm\n",
    "1. Initialize an empty result list.\n",
    "2. Start recursion with an empty string and both counters set to zero.\n",
    "3. Explore the opening bracket choice when allowed.\n",
    "4. Explore the closing bracket choice when allowed.\n",
    "5. Store completed valid combinations.\n",
    "6. Return the result."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "class Solution:\n",
    "    def generateParenthesis(self, n: int) -> list[str]:\n",
    "        result = []\n",
    "\n",
    "        def backtrack(current, open_count, close_count):\n",
    "            # Base condition\n",
    "            if len(current) == 2 * n:\n",
    "                result.append(current)\n",
    "                return\n",
    "\n",
    "            # Add opening bracket\n",
    "            if open_count < n:\n",
    "                backtrack(current + '(', open_count + 1, close_count)\n",
    "\n",
    "            # Add closing bracket\n",
    "            if close_count < open_count:\n",
    "                backtrack(current + ')', open_count, close_count + 1)\n",
    "\n",
    "        backtrack('', 0, 0)\n",
    "        return result\n",
    "\n",
    "# Test\n",
    "sol = Solution()\n",
    "print(sol.generateParenthesis(3))"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Complexity Analysis\n",
    "\n",
    "Let C_n represent the nth Catalan number, which is the number of valid combinations.\n",
    "\n",
    "- Time Complexity: O(n * C_n), because we construct and return C_n strings of length 2*n.\n",
    "- Auxiliary Space Complexity: O(n) for the recursion stack, excluding the output.\n",
    "- Output Space Complexity: O(n * C_n).\n",
    "\n",
    "### Why is Backtracking Better?\n",
    "\n",
    "It avoids generating invalid combinations. The constraints help prune unnecessary recursive calls early."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Approach 3: Dynamic Programming\n",
    "\n",
    "### Intuition\n",
    "\n",
    "We use previously generated valid combinations to construct solutions for larger values of n.\n",
    "\n",
    "Every valid combination can be represented as:\n",
    "\n",
    "'(' + A + ')' + B\n",
    "\n",
    "Where:\n",
    "- A is a valid combination containing j pairs.\n",
    "- B is a valid combination containing n - 1 - j pairs.\n",
    "\n",
    "We build the answers from dp[0] up to dp[n].\n",
    "\n",
    "### Algorithm\n",
    "1. Create a DP array of size n + 1.\n",
    "2. Set dp[0] = [''] because an empty string is one valid combination of zero pairs.\n",
    "3. Iterate from 1 to n.\n",
    "4. For each number of pairs, combine previously calculated left and right results.\n",
    "5. Return dp[n]."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "class Solution:\n",
    "    def generateParenthesis(self, n: int) -> list[str]:\n",
    "        dp = [[] for _ in range(n + 1)]\n",
    "\n",
    "        # Base case\n",
    "        dp[0] = ['']\n",
    "\n",
    "        for i in range(1, n + 1):\n",
    "            for j in range(i):\n",
    "                for left in dp[j]:\n",
    "                    for right in dp[i - 1 - j]:\n",
    "                        dp[i].append('(' + left + ')' + right)\n",
    "\n",
    "        return dp[n]\n",
    "\n",
    "# Test\n",
    "sol = Solution()\n",
    "print(sol.generateParenthesis(3))"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Complexity Analysis\n",
    "\n",
    "Let C_n be the nth Catalan number.\n",
    "\n",
    "- Time Complexity: O(n^2 * C_n) as a conservative bound when accounting for string construction and copying.\n",
    "- Space Complexity: O(n * C_n) stored combinations, excluding the cost of the characters within the strings. Including string storage, the bound is O(n^2 * C_n).\n",
    "\n",
    "### Why use Dynamic Programming?\n",
    "\n",
    "It reuses previously computed valid combinations instead of starting from scratch. It is a useful way to understand the Catalan number structure behind this problem."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Test Cases for All Three Approaches\n",
    "\n",
    "We will test each approach against the same expected results. Since different approaches can return combinations in different orders, we compare sets."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "test_cases = [\n",
    "    (1, ['()']),\n",
    "    (2, ['(())', '()()']),\n",
    "    (3, ['((()))', '(()())', '(())()', '()(())', '()()()'])\n",
    "]\n",
    "\n",
    "solutions = [\n",
    "    Solution()  # Replace with separate classes below if testing all approaches together\n",
    "]\n",
    "\n",
    "for n, expected in test_cases:\n",
    "    result = Solution().generateParenthesis(n)\n",
    "    assert set(result) == set(expected)\n",
    "    assert len(result) == len(expected)\n",
    "\n",
    "print('All test cases passed!')"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Final Comparison\n",
    "\n",
    "| Approach | Time Complexity | Auxiliary Space | Main Idea |\n",
    "|---|---|---|---|\n",
    "| Brute Force | O(n * 4^n) | O(n) | Generate and validate |\n",
    "| Backtracking | O(n * C_n) | O(n) | Generate only valid strings |\n",
    "| Dynamic Programming | O(n^2 * C_n) conservative | O(n * C_n) stored combinations | Reuse smaller solutions |\n",
    "\n",
    "## Key Learnings\n",
    "\n",
    "1. Brute force is useful for understanding the search space.\n",
    "2. Backtracking avoids invalid combinations using constraints.\n",
    "3. Dynamic Programming reuses previously calculated combinations.\n",
    "4. The number of valid combinations follows the Catalan number sequence.\n",
    "5. Backtracking is the recommended interview solution for this problem.\n",
    "\n",
    "## Final Recommendation\n",
    "\n",
    "**Best interview approach: Backtracking.**\n",
    "\n",
    "It is efficient, easy to explain, and avoids generating invalid strings."
   ]
  }
 ],
